To see the Builder Pattern in a real Spring Boot environment, we usually combine it with Lombok (to remove boilerplate) and JPA (for database entities).
Imagine you are building a User Registration system. A user must have a username and email, but their bio and phone number are optional.
1. The Maven Dependency
First, ensure you have Lombok in your pom.xml. This is the industry standard for Builders in Spring Boot.
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
    <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
    <optional>true</optional>
</dependency>

2. The Model (The Entity)
We use @Builder from Lombok. To solve the Mandatory Field problem we discussed, we create a custom static method that forces the developer to provide the "Must-have" data.
import lombok.*;
import org.springframework.util.Assert;

@Entity
@Getter 
@NoArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PROTECTED) // Required by JPA
@AllArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PRIVATE) // Required by Builder
@Builder(builderMethodName = "hiddenBuilder") // We hide the raw builder
public class AppUser {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    // MANDATORY
    private String username;
    private String email;

    // OPTIONAL
    private String phoneNumber;
    private String bio;

    // --- THE CUSTOM BUILDER GATE ---
    // This forces the developer to provide Username and Email at Compile-Time
    public static AppUserBuilder builder(String username, String email) {
        Assert.hasText(username, "Username is required!");
        Assert.hasText(email, "Email is required!");

        return hiddenBuilder()
                .username(username)
                .email(email);
    }
}

3. The Service (How you use it)
This is where you see the Method Chaining and the Safety in action.
@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    public void registerNewUser() {
        // 1. You ARE FORCED to provide username/email here (Compile-time check)
        AppUser newUser = AppUser.builder("rahul_dev", "rahul@tcs.com")
                                 .bio("Java Developer exploring Patterns") // Optional
                                 .phoneNumber("9876543210")                // Optional
                                 .build();

        // 2. Save the fully-formed, consistent object
        userRepository.save(newUser);
        
        System.out.println("User saved successfully with Builder!");
    }
}

4. Why this is the "Senior" way to code:
 * Atomic State: Notice the newUser object is only created when .build() is called. It is never "half-empty" in your Service layer.
 * Immutability: Because we used @Getter but not @Setter, once the builder creates the AppUser, no one can change its fields. This makes your code Thread-Safe.
 * Cleaner Logic: Your UserService looks like a story. You can see exactly which fields are being set without guessing the order of parameters in a huge constructor.
